--
-- Baseline schema: every migration up to and including
-- 0080_user_avatars_and_field_gates, as one file.
--
-- Generated by scripts/generate-migration-baseline.sh, which applies the old
-- migration history to an empty database and dumps the result. NOT by
-- concatenating those files: the history is a diary of tables that were built,
-- rewritten and dropped again, and replaying that diary is the thing this
-- replaces.
--
-- It is also NOT generated from packages/db/src/schema.ts. Drizzle's schema
-- describes tables and nothing else, so a baseline generated from it comes out
-- with all 65 tables and ZERO of the 87 row-level security policies. That is
-- not a degraded baseline, it is a fresh install with tenant isolation turned
-- off. The realised schema of a migrated database is the only source that has
-- all of it, which is why the generator goes through Postgres.
--
-- `SET check_function_bodies = false` below is load-bearing, not leftover dump
-- noise: pg_dump writes functions in name order, so a body that calls another
-- function is created before its callee exists.
--
-- Applying this to an existing database is prevented twice over.
-- packages/db/src/migrate.ts refuses outright if the database is behind the
-- baseline, and Drizzle then skips it anyway: it only applies a migration whose
-- journal timestamp is newer than the newest the database has recorded, and
-- this file deliberately carries the timestamp the original 0000 had. A
-- database that ran the old history is at 1787500000000 and will never run
-- this.
--

--
-- PostgreSQL database dump
--

SET check_function_bodies = false;

